Subject: [PATCH] mmc: clear single-block recovery flag for new requests

Linux 7.2 includes MQRQ_XFER_SINGLE_BLOCK, added by c7c6d4f51038 and
extended by d34124edffdb. The flag intentionally survives requeueing so a
failed multiblock request retries one block at a time, but it is never cleared
when the blk-mq tag/PDU is reused for a new request.

On OMAP3530 this eventually leaves every tag in recovery mode. A contiguous
128 KiB write then arrives at the block layer as 256 sectors but is issued as
256 CMD24 single-block writes, each followed by CMD13, yielding about 75 kB/s.
The same card, DT settings, and workload on Linux 6.6 issue one 256-block CMD25
and reach 4.9 MB/s.

Clear the flag together with retries when RQF_DONTPREP identifies a new block
request. Requeues retain RQF_DONTPREP and therefore preserve the intended
single-block recovery behavior for the failed request.
---
 drivers/mmc/core/queue.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/queue.c b/drivers/mmc/core/queue.c
--- a/drivers/mmc/core/queue.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/queue.c
@@ -293,6 +293,7 @@ static blk_status_t mmc_mq_queue_rq(struct blk_mq_hw_ctx *hctx,
 
 	if (!(req->rq_flags & RQF_DONTPREP)) {
 		req_to_mmc_queue_req(req)->retries = 0;
+		req_to_mmc_queue_req(req)->flags = 0;
 		req->rq_flags |= RQF_DONTPREP;
 	}
 
